The member-wise copy is already close to optimal, so this change is small. The common case is a message with no developer fields. In that case we skip the copy call for `devFields` and leave the vector default-constructed.

When developer fields exist, assigning them into the empty vector does one exact-size allocation, the same as a copy-construct would. The resulting `Mesg` is identical to the one the original constructor produces.

```cpp
Mesg::Mesg(const Mesg &mesg)
    : profile(mesg.profile)
    , localNum(mesg.localNum)
    , fields(mesg.fields)
    , devFields()
{
    if (!mesg.devFields.empty())
        devFields = mesg.devFields;
}
```